-- ============================================================
-- ARTICLE 1: Modbus Integration in Building Automation
-- Category: Reference
-- Sources:
--   Modbus Organization, "MODBUS Application Protocol Specification V1.1b3"
--   IANA Service Name Registry, port 502 (service name "mbap")
--   Modbus Organization, "MODBUS Messaging on TCP/IP Implementation Guide V1.0b"
-- ============================================================

INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'reference'),
  'Modbus Integration in Building Automation',
  'modbus-integration-building-automation',
  'Reference guide to Modbus RTU and Modbus TCP in BAS environments, covering register types, function codes, wiring, and integration patterns for meters, VFDs, and boilers.',
  E'# Modbus Integration in Building Automation\n\nModbus is one of the most widely encountered communication protocols in building automation. Originally published by Modicon in 1979, it is now maintained as an open standard by the Modbus Organization. BAS technicians encounter Modbus on variable frequency drives, power meters, boilers, chillers, and a wide range of third-party equipment that does not natively speak BACnet.\n\n## Protocol Variants\n\n| Variant | Transport | Typical Medium | Error Check |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Modbus RTU | Serial (RS-485 or RS-232) | Shielded twisted pair | CRC-16 |\n| Modbus ASCII | Serial (RS-485 or RS-232) | Shielded twisted pair | LRC |\n| Modbus TCP | Ethernet (TCP/IP) | Cat5e/Cat6 | TCP checksum |\n\nModbus RTU is the most common variant in field devices. Modbus TCP uses IANA-registered port 502 (service name "mbap") and wraps the same application-layer message inside a TCP packet with a Modbus Application Protocol (MBAP) header.\n\nSource: Modbus Organization, "MODBUS Application Protocol Specification V1.1b3"; IANA Service Name Registry, port 502.\n\n## Register Types\n\nModbus defines four data areas, each with a traditional address prefix:\n\n| Register Type | Address Range | Data Size | Access | Read FC | Write FC |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Coils | 0xxxx (00001–09999) | 1 bit | Read/Write | FC01 | FC05 (single), FC15 (multiple) |\n| Discrete Inputs | 1xxxx (10001–19999) | 1 bit | Read Only | FC02 | — |\n| Input Registers | 3xxxx (30001–39999) | 16 bit | Read Only | FC04 | — |\n| Holding Registers | 4xxxx (40001–49999) | 16 bit | Read/Write | FC03 | FC06 (single), FC16 (multiple) |\n\nSource: Modbus Organization, "MODBUS Application Protocol Specification V1.1b3," Tables 1 and 2.\n\n## Common Function Codes\n\n| Code | Name | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| FC01 | Read Coils | Read 1-bit outputs (ON/OFF status) |\n| FC02 | Read Discrete Inputs | Read 1-bit inputs (contact status) |\n| FC03 | Read Holding Registers | Read 16-bit read/write registers |\n| FC04 | Read Input Registers | Read 16-bit read-only registers |\n| FC05 | Write Single Coil | Set one coil ON (0xFF00) or OFF (0x0000) |\n| FC06 | Write Single Register | Write one 16-bit holding register |\n| FC15 | Write Multiple Coils | Write multiple coils in one request |\n| FC16 | Write Multiple Registers | Write multiple holding registers in one request |\n\nSource: Modbus Organization, "MODBUS Application Protocol Specification V1.1b3," Section 6.\n\n## RS-485 Wiring for Modbus RTU\n\nModbus RTU over RS-485 is a multi-drop bus:\n\n```\nWiring Requirements:\n  Topology: Daisy chain (no star or T-tap)\n  Cable: Shielded twisted pair (STP)\n  Termination: 120 ohm resistor at each end of bus\n  Max devices: 32 per segment (without repeaters)\n  Max length: 1200 m (4000 ft) at 9600 baud\n  Shield: Ground at one end only to avoid ground loops\n```\n\n### Common Baud Rates\n\n| Baud Rate | Typical Use |\n|---|---|\n| 9600 | Default for most devices |\n| 19200 | Common alternative |\n| 38400 | Higher-speed applications |\n| 115200 | Short-distance, high-speed |\n\nAll devices on the same RS-485 bus must use the same baud rate, parity, and stop bit settings.\n\n## BAS Integration Patterns\n\n### Pattern 1: BAS Controller with Modbus Driver\n\nModern BAS controllers (Niagara JACE, Metasys NAE/NIE) include Modbus drivers that poll field devices directly:\n\n```\nBAS Controller (Modbus Master)\n  ├── RS-485 Port → VFD (Slave ID 1)\n  ├── RS-485 Port → Power Meter (Slave ID 2)\n  └── Ethernet → Modbus TCP Meter (IP: 10.10.5.50)\n```\n\n### Pattern 2: Protocol Gateway\n\nA standalone gateway translates between Modbus and BACnet:\n\n```\nModbus Devices ←→ Modbus-to-BACnet Gateway ←→ BACnet/IP Network\n```\n\nGateways are useful when the BAS controller lacks a Modbus driver or when the Modbus devices are on an isolated network.\n\n## Common BAS Equipment Using Modbus\n\n| Equipment | Typical Protocol | Common Registers |\n|---|---|---|\n| VFDs | Modbus RTU (RS-485) | Speed reference, run/stop command, actual speed, fault code, motor current |\n| Power meters | Modbus RTU or TCP | Voltage, current, power (kW), energy (kWh), power factor |\n| Boilers | Modbus RTU | Firing rate, supply/return temp, fault status, enable/disable |\n| Chillers | Modbus RTU or TCP | Leaving water temp, entering water temp, capacity, fault code |\n| Lighting panels | Modbus RTU or TCP | Zone on/off, dimming level, scene selection |\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n| Symptom | Likely Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| No response from slave | Wrong slave ID, baud rate, or parity | Verify settings match device documentation |\n| CRC errors | Wiring issue, missing termination, or noise | Check termination resistors, shielding, and cable routing |\n| Timeout errors | Polling too fast, bus overloaded | Increase poll interval, reduce devices per segment |\n| Incorrect values | Wrong register address or data type | Verify register map; check byte order (big-endian vs little-endian) |\n| Intermittent communication | Loose wiring, ground loop | Re-terminate connections, ground shield at one end only |\n\n### Byte Order (Endianness)\n\nModbus registers are 16 bits, but 32-bit values (floats, long integers) span two registers. Byte order varies by manufacturer:\n\n| Order | Description | Example (float 123.456) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Big-Endian (AB CD) | Most significant word first | Register N = 0x42F6, N+1 = 0xE979 |\n| Little-Endian (CD AB) | Least significant word first | Register N = 0xE979, N+1 = 0x42F6 |\n| Mid-Big (BA DC) | Byte-swapped big-endian | Register N = 0xF642, N+1 = 0x79E9 |\n| Mid-Little (DC BA) | Byte-swapped little-endian | Register N = 0x79E9, N+1 = 0xF642 |\n\nAlways consult the device register map documentation to confirm byte order.\n\n## References\n\n- Modbus Organization, "MODBUS Application Protocol Specification V1.1b3" (modbus.org)\n- Modbus Organization, "MODBUS Messaging on TCP/IP Implementation Guide V1.0b" (modbus.org)\n- IANA Service Name and Transport Protocol Port Number Registry, port 502\n- TIA/EIA-485-A Standard for RS-485 electrical characteristics',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

