-- ============================================================
-- ARTICLE 2: BACnet Priority Array and Commandability
-- Category: Reference
-- Sources:
--   ASHRAE Standard 135-2020, Clause 19 (Priority Array property)
--   OPC Foundation, "BACnet 3.2.1 Command Prioritization"
--   Chipkin, "BACnet: What is a BACnet Priority Array?"
--   Chipkin, "BACnet Commandable versus Writable"
--   Beckhoff, "BACnet Command Prioritization"
-- ============================================================

INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'reference'),
  'BACnet Priority Array and Commandability',
  'bacnet-priority-array-commandability',
  'Reference guide to the BACnet 16-level priority array, commandable vs. writable objects, relinquish default behavior, and common field problems per ASHRAE Standard 135.',
  E'# BACnet Priority Array and Commandability\n\nThe priority array is one of the most important concepts in BACnet. It determines which command source controls a point when multiple sources (operator overrides, schedules, supervisory logic, life-safety systems) compete for the same output. Misunderstanding it is one of the most common causes of "stuck" equipment in the field.\n\n## The 16 Priority Levels\n\nASHRAE Standard 135 defines the Priority_Array as an ordered array of 16 slots. The device scans from priority 1 (highest) downward and uses the value from the highest-priority slot that holds a non-NULL value to determine the Present_Value.\n\nSix levels have standard designations; the remaining ten are available for application-specific use:\n\n| Priority | Designation | Typical Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Manual-Life Safety | Fire alarm shutdown, smoke control |\n| 2 | Automatic-Life Safety | Automatic fire/smoke response |\n| 3 | Available | — |\n| 4 | Available | — |\n| 5 | Critical Equipment Control | Freeze protection, high-limit safety |\n| 6 | Minimum On/Off | Minimum on/off time enforcement |\n| 7 | Available | — |\n| 8 | Manual Operator | Operator overrides from front-end |\n| 9 | Available | — |\n| 10 | Available | Commonly used for supervisory control |\n| 11 | Available | — |\n| 12 | Available | — |\n| 13 | Available | — |\n| 14 | Available | — |\n| 15 | Available | Commonly used by BACnet Schedule objects |\n| 16 | Available | Lowest; default when no priority is specified |\n\nSource: ASHRAE Standard 135-2020, Clause 19; OPC Foundation, "BACnet 3.2.1 Command Prioritization."\n\n## How Command Resolution Works\n\n```\nPriority Array Scan (top to bottom):\n\n  Slot 1:  NULL\n  Slot 2:  NULL\n  ...\n  Slot 8:  72.0°F  ← Operator override\n  ...\n  Slot 15: 74.0°F  ← Schedule\n  Slot 16: NULL\n\n  Result: Present_Value = 72.0°F (priority 8 wins)\n```\n\nWhen the operator clears the override (writes NULL to slot 8), the schedule at priority 15 takes over and Present_Value becomes 74.0°F.\n\n## Commandable vs. Writable\n\nASHRAE 135 distinguishes two types of properties:\n\n| Property Type | Conflict Resolution | Has Priority Array? |\n|---|---|---|\n| Writable (non-commandable) | Last write wins | No |\n| Commandable | Priority-based | Yes (required) |\n\nFor commandable properties, writes are directed to a specific priority slot. The standard requires that if a property is commandable, it must have both a Priority_Array and a Relinquish_Default property.\n\nObject types that are required to be commandable per ASHRAE 135 include Analog Output, Binary Output, Multi-state Output, Binary Lighting Output, Lighting Output, and Access Door. Analog Value and Binary Value objects may optionally be commandable.\n\nSource: Chipkin, "BACnet Commandable versus Writable"; ASHRAE Standard 135-2020.\n\n## Relinquish Default\n\nThe Relinquish_Default property specifies the fallback value for Present_Value when all 16 priority slots contain NULL. This ensures a predictable default state when no active command exists.\n\n```\nExample: Binary Output controlling a fan\n  Relinquish_Default: OFF (inactive)\n\n  If all 16 slots are NULL → fan is OFF\n  If slot 15 (schedule) = ON → fan is ON\n  If schedule writes NULL to slot 15 → fan returns to OFF\n```\n\nPer ASHRAE 135, if either Relinquish_Default or Priority_Array is present, both must be present.\n\nSource: ASHRAE Standard 135-2020, Clause 12.\n\n## Convention: Schedules and Supervisory Control\n\nBy convention in the BAS industry:\n\n| Priority | Conventional Use |\n|---|---|\n| 1–2 | Life safety (fire, smoke) |\n| 5 | Critical equipment protection |\n| 8 | Operator manual overrides |\n| 10 | Supervisory / BAS control logic |\n| 15 | BACnet Schedule objects |\n| 16 | Default / fallback |\n\nThis layering means a schedule (15) yields to supervisory logic (10), which yields to an operator override (8), which yields to critical equipment control (5), which yields to life safety (1–2). When a higher-priority command is cleared, control falls back to the next active slot.\n\nSource: Schneider Electric, "Configuring the BACnet Command Priority Level for Forced Values"; Beckhoff, "BACnet Command Prioritization."\n\n## Common Field Problems\n\n### Sticky Overrides\n\nValues written to a priority slot persist until explicitly relinquished by writing NULL to that slot. Operators who forget to relinquish leave stale commands that override normal control indefinitely.\n\n```\nSymptom: VAV damper stuck at 100% open\nCause: Technician wrote to priority 8 during commissioning,\n       never wrote NULL to clear it\nFix: Write NULL to the affected priority slot\n```\n\n### Override Lockout\n\nA forgotten command at a high priority prevents lower-priority control logic from taking effect. The equipment appears "stuck" even though the control program is running correctly.\n\n### Last-Write-Wins at Same Priority\n\nIf two clients write to the same priority slot, the most recent write overwrites the previous one. The first client to relinquish (write NULL) clears the slot entirely, including the second client''s value.\n\n### Default Priority Trap\n\nWhen no priority is specified in a BACnet write, many implementations default to priority 16. Multiple writers using priority 16 silently overwrite each other with no awareness of the conflict.\n\n## Diagnostic Checklist\n\nWhen a point appears stuck or unresponsive to control logic:\n\n```\nTroubleshooting Steps:\n  1. Read the Priority_Array property — identify which slot holds a non-NULL value\n  2. Identify the source that wrote to that slot\n  3. If the value is stale, write NULL to that slot to clear the override\n  4. Verify Present_Value now follows the expected control source\n  5. Check Relinquish_Default is set to a safe fallback value\n```\n\n## References\n\n- ASHRAE Standard 135-2020: BACnet — A Data Communication Protocol for Building Automation and Control Networks\n- OPC Foundation, "BACnet 3.2.1 Command Prioritization" (reference.opcfoundation.org)\n- Chipkin, "BACnet: What is a BACnet Priority Array?" (store.chipkin.com)\n- Chipkin, "BACnet Commandable versus Writable" (store.chipkin.com)\n- Beckhoff, "BACnet Command Prioritization" (infosys.beckhoff.com)\n- Schneider Electric, "Configuring the BACnet Command Priority Level" (ecostruxure-building-help.se.com)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

