-- ============================================================
-- ARTICLE 3: Variable Frequency Drive (VFD) Integration with BAS
-- Category: How-To
-- Sources:
--   Modbus Organization, "MODBUS Application Protocol Specification V1.1b3"
--   ASHRAE Standard 135-2020 (BACnet object types for VFD monitoring)
--   NFPA 70 National Electrical Code, Article 430 (motor circuits)
--   ASHRAE Handbook — HVAC Applications, Chapter 48 (Noise and Vibration Control)
-- ============================================================

INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'how-to'),
  'Variable Frequency Drive (VFD) Integration with BAS',
  'vfd-integration-with-bas',
  'Practical guide to integrating VFDs with building automation systems using BACnet and Modbus, covering communication setup, common parameters, wiring, and troubleshooting.',
  E'# Variable Frequency Drive (VFD) Integration with BAS\n\nVariable frequency drives are present on nearly every modern BAS project — fans, pumps, cooling towers, and compressors all benefit from variable-speed control. Integrating VFDs with the BAS via a network protocol eliminates hardwired analog signals and gives operators visibility into drive status, faults, and energy consumption.\n\n## Communication Protocol Options\n\nMost VFD manufacturers offer multiple communication interfaces:\n\n| Protocol | Interface | Typical Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Modbus RTU | RS-485 (built-in on most drives) | Most common BAS integration method |\n| Modbus TCP | Ethernet (built-in or plug-in card) | IP-based integration |\n| BACnet MS/TP | RS-485 (plug-in card or built-in) | Native BACnet integration |\n| BACnet/IP | Ethernet (plug-in card) | IP-based BACnet integration |\n| LON (FT-10) | Twisted pair (plug-in card) | Legacy installations |\n| EtherNet/IP | Ethernet (plug-in card) | Industrial / process applications |\n\nModbus RTU over RS-485 is the most common integration method because most VFDs include a built-in RS-485 port at no additional cost.\n\nSource: Manufacturer documentation from ABB, Danfoss, Yaskawa, and Siemens all confirm built-in Modbus RTU as a standard feature.\n\n## Common BAS-to-VFD Parameters\n\n### Commands (BAS → VFD)\n\n| Parameter | Data Type | Register Type | Description |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Run/Stop Command | Bit | Coil or Holding Register | Start or stop the motor |\n| Speed Reference | 16-bit integer | Holding Register | Frequency setpoint (typically 0–100% or 0–60 Hz) |\n| Fault Reset | Bit | Coil | Clear a latched fault |\n| Forward/Reverse | Bit | Coil | Direction of rotation |\n\n### Status (VFD → BAS)\n\n| Parameter | Data Type | Register Type | Description |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Running Status | Bit | Discrete Input or Holding Register | Motor is running |\n| Actual Speed | 16-bit integer | Input Register | Current output frequency (Hz) or percent |\n| Motor Current | 16-bit integer | Input Register | Measured amps (often scaled by 10 or 100) |\n| Motor Voltage | 16-bit integer | Input Register | Output voltage |\n| Drive Temperature | 16-bit integer | Input Register | Heatsink temperature |\n| Fault Code | 16-bit integer | Input Register | Active fault number (0 = no fault) |\n| kWh Counter | 32-bit (2 registers) | Input Register | Accumulated energy consumption |\n\nRegister addresses vary by manufacturer and model. Always consult the specific VFD Modbus register map.\n\n## Modbus RTU Wiring\n\n```\nRS-485 Wiring (BAS Controller to VFD):\n\n  BAS Controller          VFD 1            VFD 2            VFD 3\n  ┌──────────┐     ┌──────────┐     ┌──────────┐     ┌──────────┐\n  │ RS-485 + ├─────┤ RS-485 + ├─────┤ RS-485 + ├─────┤ RS-485 + │\n  │ RS-485 - ├─────┤ RS-485 - ├─────┤ RS-485 - ├─────┤ RS-485 - │\n  │ Shield   ├─────┤ Shield   ├─────┤ Shield   ├─────┤ Shield   │\n  └──────────┘     └──────────┘     └──────────┘     └──────────┘\n       120Ω                                               120Ω\n   (termination)                                     (termination)\n```\n\n### Wiring Best Practices\n\n- Use shielded twisted pair cable rated for RS-485\n- Daisy-chain topology only — no star or T-tap connections\n- Terminate with 120 ohm resistors at each end of the bus\n- Ground the shield at one end only to prevent ground loops\n- Route RS-485 cable away from VFD power cables (minimum 300 mm / 12 in separation)\n- Do not route communication cables through the same conduit as motor power cables\n\nSource: TIA/EIA-485-A standard for RS-485 electrical characteristics; VFD manufacturer installation guides universally require physical separation from power conductors.\n\n## VFD Configuration Checklist\n\nBefore commissioning the network integration:\n\n```\nDrive-Side Configuration:\n  [ ] Set communication protocol (Modbus RTU, BACnet, etc.)\n  [ ] Set slave/device address (unique on the bus)\n  [ ] Set baud rate to match BAS controller\n  [ ] Set parity and stop bits to match BAS controller\n  [ ] Set command source to "Network" (not local keypad)\n  [ ] Set speed reference source to "Network"\n  [ ] Configure communication loss timeout and fallback action\n  [ ] Verify drive accepts network run command\n  [ ] Verify drive reports actual speed on network\n\nBAS-Side Configuration:\n  [ ] Add Modbus device with correct slave ID\n  [ ] Configure register addresses per VFD register map\n  [ ] Set data type and scaling factors\n  [ ] Set polling interval (typically 5–10 seconds)\n  [ ] Map fault code to BAS alarm\n  [ ] Test run/stop command\n  [ ] Verify speed reference tracks setpoint\n```\n\n## Communication Loss Behavior\n\nConfigure what happens if the VFD loses communication with the BAS:\n\n| Behavior | Setting | When to Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stop | Fault on comm loss | Critical applications where uncontrolled operation is unsafe |\n| Hold last speed | Coast at current speed | Applications that can safely continue at current speed |\n| Run at preset speed | Fallback to fixed frequency | Applications needing guaranteed minimum airflow |\n| Revert to local | Switch to keypad/analog control | Dual-control installations with hardwired backup |\n\nCommunication loss timeout is typically configurable from 1 to 60 seconds.\n\n## Common Fault Codes\n\nFault codes vary by manufacturer, but common categories include:\n\n| Category | Typical Code Range | BAS Alarm Priority |\n|---|---|---|\n| Overcurrent | Hardware trip | Critical |\n| Overvoltage | DC bus voltage too high | Critical |\n| Undervoltage | DC bus voltage too low | Warning |\n| Overtemperature | Drive heatsink or motor temp | Critical |\n| Communication loss | Network timeout | Warning |\n| Ground fault | Motor winding to ground | Critical |\n| Motor overload | Thermal model trip | Critical |\n| External fault | External interlock open | Warning |\n\nMap critical faults to BAS alarms for operator notification.\n\n## Scaling Considerations\n\nVFD registers often use integer scaling. Common patterns:\n\n| Parameter | Register Value | Actual Value | Scale Factor |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Speed (Hz) | 3000 | 30.00 Hz | ÷ 100 |\n| Current (A) | 125 | 12.5 A | ÷ 10 |\n| Voltage (V) | 4600 | 460 V | ÷ 10 |\n| Power (kW) | 750 | 7.50 kW | ÷ 100 |\n| Speed reference (%) | 5000 | 50.00% | ÷ 100 |\n\nAlways verify the scale factor from the manufacturer register map.\n\n## References\n\n- Modbus Organization, "MODBUS Application Protocol Specification V1.1b3" (modbus.org)\n- ASHRAE Standard 135-2020: BACnet object types for motor/drive monitoring\n- TIA/EIA-485-A: Electrical Characteristics of Generators and Receivers for Balanced Digital Multipoint Systems\n- NFPA 70, National Electrical Code, Article 430: Motor Circuits\n- ABB, Danfoss, Yaskawa, Siemens VFD communication guides (manufacturer documentation)',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

